Month = {DEC 31},
Abstract = {The use of steel fibres in reinforcing concrete has proven to enhance
   certain mechanical and durability properties of concrete; however, as a
   material that presents enhanced properties, its environmental
   performance should also be analysed. This study aims to quantify the
   carbon emissions of steel fibre-reinforced concrete beams using a
   functional unit that considers the mechanical and durability performance
   of concrete through a whole life cycle assessment methodology that
   includes the benefits/load after the end-of-life. A cradle-to-grave
   approach, which considers the end-of-life stage and the benefits/loads
   beyond the system boundary, was performed to compare the embodied carbon
   of conventionally reinforced concrete and steel fibre-reinforced
   concrete beams. The results show that the addition of steel fibres as
   reinforcing material to concrete can reduce the area of steel required
   in the tension zone of a conventionally reinforced concrete beam and the
   embodied carbon of concrete by an average of 33\% and 37\%,
   respectively.},
